  "landfallEstimate": "Movement towards the coast",
  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES,\nNEW DELHI TROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO 16\nDEMS-RSMC TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 18.11.2026\nFROM: RSMC \u2013TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTO: STORM WARNING CENTRE, NAYPYITAW (MYANMAR)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, BANGKOK (THAILAND)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, COLOMBO (SRILANKA)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, DHAKA (BANGLADESH)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, KARACHI (PAKISTAN)\nMETEOROLOGICAL OFFICE, MALE (MALDIVES)\nOMAN METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT,\nMUSCAT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nYEMEN METEOROLOGICAL SERVICES,\nREPUBLIC OF YEMEN (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nNATIONAL CENTRE FOR METEOROLOGY, UAE (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nPRESIDENCY OF METEOROLOGY AND ENVIRONMENT,\nSAUDI ARABIA (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nIRAN METEOROLOGICAL ORGANISATION, (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nQATAR METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 16 FOR THE NORTH INDIAN OCEAN\n(THE BAY OF BENGAL AND THE ARABIAN SEA) VALID FOR THE NEXT 168\nHOURS ISSUED AT 1200 UTC OF 18.11.2026 BASED ON 0900 UTC OF\n18.11.2026.\nSub: Severe Cyclonic Storm Akvan (pronounced \u201cAkvan\u201d) over Westcentral\nand adjoining central parts of South Bay of Bengal\nThe Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral and\nadjoining central parts of south Bay of Bengal moved north-northwestwards with the speed\nof 08 kmph during past 06 hours and lay centered at 0900 UTC of today, the 18th\nNovember, 2026 over the same region, near Latitude 13.3\u00b0N and Longitude 85.3\u00b0E, about\n520 km southeast of Narsapur (43187, Andhra Pradesh), 560 km east of Chennai (43279,\nTamil Nadu), 570 km south-southeast of Kalingapatnam (43105, Andhra Pradesh), 660 km\nsouth of Gopalpur (43049, Odisha) and 720 km south of Puri (43053, Odisha).\nContinuing to move north-northwestward, it is likely to cross North Andhra Pradesh & Yanam\nand South Odisha coasts between Narsapur and Puri, by 0600 UTC of 20th November,\n2026 as a severe cyclonic storm with the maximum sustained wind speed of 90-100 kmph\ngusting to 110 kmph.\nCloud distribution: (a) Isolated: <25%, Scattered:25-50%, Broken: 51-75%, Solid:>75%, Convection Intensity: (a) Weak:\nCloud Top Temperature(CTT)>-25\u00b0C, (b) Moderate: CTT:-25\u00b0Cto-40\u00b0C, (c) Intense: CTT: -41\u00b0Cto -70\u00b0C and (d) Very\nIntense::Less than -70\u00b0C Probability of cyclogenesis (formation of depression) :NIL:0%, LOW:1-33%, MODERATE:34-\n66% and HIGH:67-100%\nThe forecast location and intensity of the system is given below:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. surface disturbance\n0E) wind speed (Kmph)\n18.11.26/0900 13.3/85.3 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n18.11.26/1200 13.7/85.2 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n18.11.26/1800 14.1/85.1 100-110 gusting to 120 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/0000 15.0/84.9 100-110 gusting to 120 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/0600 16.0/84.8 100-110 gusting to 120 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/1800 17.6/84.7 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0600 19.1/84.7 80-90 gusting to 100 Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/1800 20.7/84.9 40-50 gusting to 60 Depression\n21.11.26/0600 22.4/85.2 25-35 gusting to 45 Well Marked Low\nPressure Area\nAs per INSAT 3DS, vortex (AKVAN) lay over the Westcentral and adjoining Central parts of\nSouth Bay of Bengal and its neighbourhood centered near 13.3N/85.7E. Intensity of the\nsystem is characterized as T3.0. The System shows shear pattern with convective clouds\nsheared towards northern sector. Associated scattered to broken low and medium-level\nclouds, with embedded intense to very intense convection lay over Central, North and\nsouthwest Bay of Bengal (minimum cloud-top temperature (CTT) ranges from -70\u00b0C to -\n90\u00b0C) and moderate to intense convection over Odisha, Gangetic West Bengal, north\nCostal Andhra Pradesh, Rayalaseema, southeast Bay of Bengal, Andaman Sea and\nIslands (minimum cloud-top temperature (CTT) ranges from -40\u00b0C to -70\u00b0C)\nThe estimated central pressure is 998 hPa and maximum sustained wind speed is 50 kt\ngusting to 60 kt.",
